ramdocs-0128-a0-conflict,ramdocs_a,What political party does Marcoux belong to?,misinfo,,,Liberal-Progressives,,Conservative,,,,0,,,Liberal-Progressives,correct,,,,"in l'Association des Commissaires d'écoles Canadiens-Français du Manitoba. He was also a member of the Laurier Club and the Canadian Club. He was a Liberal by background, and became a member of the Liberal-Progressives following the merger of the two constituent parties. In 1920, Marcoux married Eloria Normandeau. He was first elected to the Manitoba legislature in the 1936 provincial election, defeating Conservative candidate Joseph Hamelin by 830 votes in the La Verendrye constituency. He was appointed to Bracken's cabinet on September 27, 1936 as a minister without portfolio, representing the interests of Manitoba's francophone community. Marcoux was re-elected in",
ramdocs-0128-a0-conflict,ramdocs_a,What political party does Marcoux belong to?,misinfo,,,Liberal-Progressives,,Conservative,,,,1,,,Liberal-Progressives,correct,,,,", Stuart Garson and Douglas Campbell . [ 1 ] The son of Leon Marcoux and Adeline Ferland , Marcoux was born in Lorette , Manitoba , was educated in Lorette and St. Boniface , and worked as a farmer . He served as reeve for the Rural Municipality of Taché and was an active member of l'Association des Canadiens-Français du Manitoba and in l'Association des Commissaires d'écoles Canadiens-Français du Manitoba . He was also a member of the Laurier Club and the Canadian Club . He was a Liberal by background , and became a member of the Liberal-Progressives",
